---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ShopButtons from '../../components/ShopButtons.astro';
import CoachTip from '../../components/CoachTip.astro';
import { HAT_CHART, frac } from '../../lib/sizing';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Does my kid need their own batting helmet?',
    a: 'Many leagues keep team helmets in the dugout, but a shared helmet rarely fits every kid well, and a helmet that wobbles doesn’t protect properly. Your own helmet also means it’s always the right size and always clean.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a batting helmet need a face guard?',
    a: 'Some leagues require one and many don’t, but we recommend one for young players. A full face guard (cage) or a C-flap jaw guard protects against the pitches and foul tips that cause the worst youth injuries.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which side does a C-flap go on?',
    a: 'The side facing the pitcher. Right-handed batters wear it on the left side (sold as “for right-handed batters”). Left-handed batters wear it on the right side.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I put stickers on a batting helmet?',
    a: 'Check the helmet maker’s instructions first. Some paints, solvents and stickers can weaken the shell and void the NOCSAE certification.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When should a helmet be replaced?',
    a: 'Right away if the shell is cracked or the padding is loose or crushed. Otherwise, when your kid outgrows it or the maker’s recommended life is up. Many helmets print a date inside.',
  },
];

const sources = [
  { label: 'Little League®: what to wear and bring (helmets must be NOCSAE approved)', href: 'https://www.littleleague.org/university/articles/get-the-gear-and-what-to-wear-for-little-league-practices/' },
  { label: 'NOCSAE: National Operating Committee on Standards for Athletic Equipment', href: 'https://nocsae.org/' },
];
---
<GuideLayout
  slug="batting-helmet-size-guide"
  metaTitle="Youth Batting Helmet Size Chart: How to Measure (and Pick a Face Guard)"
  description="Measure your kid’s head in 30 seconds, convert it to a hat size, check the fit, and choose the right face guard or C-flap side."
  faqs={faqs}
  sources={sources}
  related={['youth-baseball-gear-checklist', 'catchers-gear-for-kids', 'tee-ball-gear']}
>
  <Fragment slot="answer">
    <p>
      Wrap a soft tape measure (or a string) around your kid’s head about <strong>an inch above the eyebrows</strong>, at the widest part. Compare that number to the
      helmet maker’s chart. Most kids 5–12 fit a <strong>Junior/youth</strong> helmet. Make sure it has the <strong>NOCSAE</strong> stamp, two ear flaps, and ideally a
      face guard.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="measure">How to measure (30 seconds)</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Use a soft tape measure. No tape? Use a string or phone charger cable, then lay it against a ruler.</li>
    <li>Wrap it around the head about an inch above the eyebrows, just above the ears, around the widest part of the back of the head.</li>
    <li>Keep it snug but not tight. Measure twice and use the larger number.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="chart">Head size to hat size chart</h2>
  <p>
    Most helmet charts list both head circumference and fitted hat size. Hat size is just the circumference divided by about 3.14. Brands label their size ranges
    differently, so use this to understand the numbers, then check the specific helmet’s chart.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Head (inches)</th><th scope="col">Hat size</th><th scope="col">Usually fits</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          HAT_CHART.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{frac(r.circ)}″</td>
              <th scope="row">{frac(r.hat)}</th>
              <td>{r.category}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="fit">The fit check</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Shake test:</strong> have them shake their head “yes” and “no.” The helmet should move with their head, not slide around.</li>
    <li><strong>Brim:</strong> the front sits about one finger-width above the eyebrows.</li>
    <li><strong>Ears:</strong> the ear holes line up with their ears.</li>
    <li><strong>Vision:</strong> in their batting stance, they can see the pitcher without tipping the helmet back.</li>
  </ul>
  <CoachTip>
    <p>Adjustable helmets (with a dial or sliding pads) are great for kids between sizes, and can stretch an extra season out of a helmet.</p>
  </CoachTip>

  <h2 id="requirements">What a youth helmet must have</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>NOCSAE stamp.</strong> Little League requires NOCSAE-approved batting helmets. Look for the seal on the back of the shell.</li>
    <li><strong>Two ear flaps.</strong> Youth leagues require double ear-flap helmets.</li>
    <li><strong>Good condition.</strong> No cracks, no loose or crushed padding.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="face-guard">Face guards and C-flaps</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">If your kid bats…</th><th scope="col">Get</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Right-handed</th><td>A full face guard, or a C-flap on the <strong>left</strong> side (“for right-handed batters”)</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Left-handed</th><td>A full face guard, or a C-flap on the <strong>right</strong> side (“for left-handed batters”)</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Both, or not sure yet</th><td>A full face guard. It covers both sides.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>For kids 8 and under we suggest a full face guard. Many youth helmets come with one already attached.</p>

  <h2 id="shop">Shop helmets</h2>
  <ShopButtons query="youth batting helmet with face guard" what="youth helmets with face guards" item="guide-helmet" />
</GuideLayout>
